// Test 2: a dictionary is addressed by KEY — positional access was withdrawn.
//
// This file used to be called "Positional Indexing on Named Tuples (Backward
// Compatibility)" and asserted that `person[1]`, `person[2]` and `person[3]`
// worked. Decision 11 of Divergente_ES/forma/README.md withdrew exactly that:
// in a mutable dictionary a positional index is fragile, because adding a key
// changes what sits at each position and a program that depended on it stops
// being correct with nothing to say so.
//
// The refusal itself is pinned in reject/collections/02_positional_read_dict.zy.
// What is kept here is the half that survives: the dot and the bracket reach the
// same value, and the bracket also reaches keys the dot cannot spell.
//
// The POSITIONAL tuple keeps `t[1]` in full — there the index is the only
// address there is, and the size is fixed. That is corpus/collections/, not here.

person = #(name: "Alice", age: 25, city: "Madrid")

? person["name"] == "Alice" {
    >> "✓ key access works" ¶
}

? person["age"] == 25 {
    >> "✓ any value type" ¶
}

// The dot and the bracket are the same lookup.
? person.name == person["name"] {
    >> "✓ dot and bracket agree" ¶
}

// A computed key — the piece that made this a dictionary rather than a record.
k = "city"
? person[k] == "Madrid" {
    >> "✓ computed key works" ¶
}

// The bracket reaches keys the dot cannot: any string is a key.
raro = #(nombre: "Ana")
raro["dos palabras"]$~ 7
? raro["dos palabras"] == 7 {
    >> "✓ a key the dot cannot spell" ¶
}

>> "Test 2 PASSED" ¶
